WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.519
In June 2015, I went to Sheffield, and I saw
a paternoster lift,

00:00:03.519 --> 00:00:07.730
an obsolete design of elevator with
a looping chain of open cabs.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.100
In June 2016, I travelled to Genoa in Italy,

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.230
and I saw an elevator that also moved sideways.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:14.090
Except, spoiler, it's a bit of a cheat,

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:15.510
it's actually a little horizontal car

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.550
that moves inside a bigger elevator that
does the heavy lifting.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.710
And now, it is June 2017.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:24.660
And I'm in Germany, at the brand-new
Thyssenkrupp Elevator Test Tower,

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.710
and they have flown me out here
to show off Multi:

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:31.269
their actual, full-size, working elevator

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:33.290
that moves sideways without cheating.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:38.070
For us, it was very important to have a tower
to test the new technology.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.820
We don't want to test it
at the customers' site.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:46.520
The tower is 246m high,
and this means 1,700 steps.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.400
I know that exactly, because I walked up once!

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:53.420
This is a building made almost entirely of
elevator shafts.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.329
Some of them test regular lifts,

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:57.900
but some of them are reserved
for this new system.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.870
The principle of having
a continuous rotating flow of cars

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.139
was one of the best inventions
in our industry ever.

00:01:07.139 --> 00:01:14.159
And to achieve this, we have to exchange cabins
from one shaft to another.

00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:16.939
And to have this exchange, we need an exchanger,

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:18.950
which you see just behind me.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.859
And this exchanger allows us
to go into horizontal movement.

00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:25.799
The exchanger can be placed at every point
in the shaft

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:30.599
so it allows us to do all kinds of configurations.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.409
Coming down vertically, and then going into
the horizontal movement.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.340
One of the problems with elevators
in really tall buildings

00:01:38.349 --> 00:01:42.320
is that you need a cable so long
that it can't support its own weight,

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.109
let alone the weight of the cab that's then
connected to it.

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:48.609
The solution here is a bit different, and
it doesn't use cables at all.

00:01:48.609 --> 00:01:51.060
It uses linear induction motors.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:58.539
Linear drive - it's also a synchronous motor
with permanent magnets.

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:01.929
And you maybe know it from a rotating motor.

00:02:01.929 --> 00:02:06.119
If we just take this motor
and cut it on one side

00:02:06.119 --> 00:02:09.399
and just lay it on a table flat,

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:11.680
then you already have a linear drive.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.959
And this is what we have,
vertical and also horizontal.

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:18.239
In a regular elevator,
it's called a traction elevator,

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:20.340
you've got cables holding the cab,

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.750
any one of which can hold
more than the design weight.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.189
Also, you've got emergency brakes
which will pop out

00:02:25.189 --> 00:02:28.280
and slow the car down with friction
if it's moving too fast.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.410
And, absolute worst case, even if all that
somehow fails,

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:35.159
you have a buffer at the bottom that should
reduce a freefall drop

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.620
from "fatal" to just "severe injuries"(!)

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:42.879
Elevators are incredibly safe because they're
incredibly well-regulated.

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:45.219
So before this is allowed to carry humans,

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.969
this system needs to be at least as failsafe.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.299
The safety is not just in the ropes
of a conventional elevator

00:02:52.299 --> 00:02:53.900
but in the brake system.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.989
In conventional elevators, you have the brake

00:02:56.989 --> 00:03:00.400
on the machine on top of the shaft in the
machine room

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.489
now we have the brake directly on the cars.

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:05.269
This new component, the exchanger,

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:09.760
where we go from vertical
into horizontal movement,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.620
also needs to be checked by our safety controller.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.440
What is the position of the exchanger?

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.499
Is it locked?

00:03:17.499 --> 00:03:18.790
Is it safe?

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:23.760
And in case of any violation
of a safety rule,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.000
then we go to a 'safe state'.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.580
There are, of course, some catches.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.189
Linear induction motors are
expensive and heavy.

00:03:31.189 --> 00:03:33.349
Each one of the cabs
needs to have its own set.

00:03:33.349 --> 00:03:35.920
And the whole elevator shaft,
from top to bottom,

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.040
needs to be lined with the magnetic coil units
that power them.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.040
To save weight, these cabs are made
of carbon fibre and aluminium.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.780
So will it work out in the real world?

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.340
Thyssenkrupp's models say it will,

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.219
but everything's been
tested and approved here,

00:03:48.219 --> 00:03:50.349
the next step is to sell it.

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:53.920
To the kind of enormous multinational companies
who build skyscrapers

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:55.689
and who do not like risk.

00:03:55.689 --> 00:04:00.300
And that might be a greater challenge than
making the elevators in the first place.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.909
Thank you very much to all the team at Thyssenkrupp
who've invited me over here.

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:06.049
You can pull down the description
for more about Multi.

